In the valley where rivers forget their names, there lived a sculptor named Lira who could hear stone breathe. On nights when the moon wore a silver fish, she would listen to the obsidian cliffs and learn their patient stories. The cliffs spoke of a circle of old stars that once danced in the sky, trading light for memory. They warned that memory, once spoken aloud, could melt into wind and vanish.

One evening, a fox with eyes like polished jet pressed its paw into a clay old-world tablet Lira kept for dust and dreams. The fox whispered a dare: carve a statue that could listen as well as speak. Lira carved a boy sitting beneath a tree, his eyes closed, his chest a quiet bell. When she finished, the statue stirred and breathed a sigh that smelled of rain and iron. “I am listening to the world’s losses,” he told her. “If you tell me what you have forgotten, I will keep it safe.”

Lira spoke of a garden where each flower remembered a name it had forgotten, of a grandmother’s soft hands, of footsteps on a road that led nowhere and everywhere. The statue nodded, and the valley grew lighter. But memory, once named, began to travel—through wind, through streams, through the fox’s clever paw—so that no heart could hold it all. Yet every listener learned to listen a little longer.
